---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Buford, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, septic backup, or storm water in Buford, GA 30518 and 30519. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Buford" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can about
      the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Buford</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Buford</h2>
    <p>
      Buford is two places. On one side is the mall: Mall of Georgia, Friendship
      Road, and I-985. When people here say "the Mall," they mean Mall of
      Georgia. That side is mostly houses, townhomes, and apartments built from
      the late 1990s on. On the other side is Lake Lanier and Buford Dam, with
      older cottages from the 1960s and newer custom houses on the shoreline.
      Old Buford, around Main Street, is older still.
    </p>
    <p>
      Houses on the mall side are now 15 to 30 years old. That is the age when
      the water heater, the washer hose, and the refrigerator line start to
      fail. So the usual call there is a leak, not a flood.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lake side is different. The houses vary a lot in age, and the oldest
      cottages may have older plumbing than anything built since. Many
      waterfront lots are on septic, not city sewer. That matters, because a
      backup is a different problem and a different first call.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is under the house matters too. In the mall corridor you find both
      slab houses and houses with basements. On the hills near the lake, a
      walkout basement or a crawl space is common. In Old Buford, older houses
      more often sit over a crawl space.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. Tell us if the house has a basement, a crawl space,
      or a slab. If it is on septic, say that too. If you are not sure, say so.
      That is enough to start.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage. In a January 2022 storm, trees and wires came down
      on roads across Buford, and one landed on a house. A damaged roof can leak
      into the attic for days before the stain shows up on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>Buford has two ZIP codes, 30518 and 30519.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Mall of Georgia and Friendship Road.</strong> Houses, townhomes, and
      apartments from the late 1990s through the 2010s. This is the busiest side,
      and many people here know exactly where the Mall is.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ivy Creek and the greenway near the Mall.</strong> Apartments and
      houses from the 2000s on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Old Buford and Main Street.</strong> Older cottages and mill-town
      houses, plus new homes built in around them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Lake Lanier and Buford Dam.</strong> Cottages from the 1960s up to
      new custom houses. Many are on septic.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Rest Haven.</strong> Older houses on the Hall County line, outside
      the city.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Buford</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the late 1990s through the 2010s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shared-wall leak.</strong> In a townhome, a neighbor's leak can come
        through the wall you share.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Septic backup.</strong> On a lake lot, wastewater can back up into a
        tub or floor drain. This is sewage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Old pipes.</strong> Older cottages may have pipe that has been in the
        walls for decades. See the question below.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm water.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a door, or a
        roof damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Buford homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>Our house is on septic and sewage is coming up in the tub. Who do we call?</dt>
        <dd>
          Stay out of the water and do not use the plumbing. Sewage is dirty
          water, and it should not be cleaned up by hand. You need two calls. A
          septic company has to find out why it backed up. Call us for the part
          inside the house, such as soaked floors, drywall, and carpet. Tell us it
          is septic when you call.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          Water is coming up in the street near my house. Do I call the city or
          the county?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          It depends on who sends your water bill. The City of Buford has its own
          water department, and some parcels on the edge of town are served by
          Gwinnett County. Check your bill. If water is also inside your house,
          shut off the main valve, take photos, and call us about the wet part
          inside.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          We are only at the lake house on weekends. How do we keep a leak from
          running all week?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Shut off the main valve when you leave. That one step stops most leaks
          from running while no one is there. Turn the water heater to vacation
          mode or off, and ask someone to check the house if you are away for a
          long time. If you come back to wet floors, take photos before you move
          anything and call.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          My townhome neighbor had a leak and water is coming through the shared
          wall. What do I do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Take photos of the wall and floor on your side, and write down when you
          first saw it. Tell your neighbor and your management company or HOA, in
          writing. Do not open the wall yourself. Water can sit inside a shared
          wall long after the wall looks dry. Call, and tell us it is a shared
          wall.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>My cottage was built in the 1960s. What is in the walls?</dt>
        <dd>
          It depends on the house. Older cottages may have copper, older steel
          pipe, or gray plastic pipe, and some may have been repiped. You cannot
          tell by looking at the neighborhood. Find the main shut-off, make sure
          it turns, and look at the pipes near the water heater. If you are not
          sure, ask a plumber.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer or septic backup. Say which one
      it is on the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="buford" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/suwanee">Suwanee</a> ·
      <a href="/hamilton-mill">Hamilton Mill</a> ·
      <a href="/dacula">Dacula</a> ·
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
